  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "id": "mechanism_1",
      "name": "strain rate hardening",
      "category": "strengthening",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "machined chip",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 8,
          "section": "4 Discussion",
          "quote": "the increase in cutting forces observed at speeds between 5 m/min and 50 m/min is very likely a result of increased material ﬂow stress associated with increased strain rate hardening.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "0a1c0ef5d6aa94ea:part:001"
        },
        {
          "page": "11",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "In this region, strain rate hardening predominates thermal softening and the material’s hardness in the shear zone is increased.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "0a1c0ef5d6aa94ea:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_2",
      "name": "thermal softening",
      "category": "softening",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "machined chip",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 8,
          "section": "4 Discussion",
          "quote": "it is clear that thermal softening begins to dominate strain rate hardening at cutting speeds above 50 m/min because both the hardness of the deformed material and the cutting forces begin to decrease beyond this critical speed.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "0a1c0ef5d6aa94ea:part:001"
        },
        {
          "page": "9",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "The dependency of the ﬂow stress on temperature for LAM tests is expected considering that all cutting temperatures are above the critical 800 ◦C, and therefore, thermal softening is expected to dominate over strain hardening at all speeds.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "0a1c0ef5d6aa94ea:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_3",
      "name": "adiabatic shear banding",
      "category": "deformation",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "machined chip",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 8,
          "section": "4 Discussion",
          "quote": "The transition from continuous to segmented chips with increasing cutting speed is commonly understood to occur when the rate of thermal softening exceeds the rate of strain hardening during plastic deformation, resulting in localised deformation along the adiabatic shear plane.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "0a1c0ef5d6aa94ea:part:001"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
